  "query_preview": "..\nThrough Thursday...\n\nThe main portion of our current weather maker has come to a close\nas the surface feature has moved off to the east. The upper level\nlow continues to push through MT resulting in some isolated to\nscattered snow showers in the area of broad ascent with good \nlapse rates. Brief periods of snow and reduced visibility are \npossible with any shower but conditions will not last long. The \nother thing to watch for tonight is patchy fog. While widespread \nfog is not expected due to the main flow being westerly, and thus \ndownslope, patchy fog in the foothills is still possible. \nLocations that saw accumulating snow during the day today have the\nhighest chance, however, these locations will also be some of the\nmost impacted by the downslope flow which means that any fog that\ndoes form will not be of any great concern. \n\nDuring the day today there is still a chance for some isolated \nsnow showers. Although the bulk of the energy associated with the\ntrough will be well over the great plans by midday today, a small\nlobe of enhanced vorticity will race its way out of the AB \nrockies and into MT. This wil be enough forcing to produce \nscattered snow showers, especially in locations where terrain aids\nin lift. Over western portions of the CWA model soundings are \nshowing nearly 9 C/KM lapse rates from the sfc to the EL this \nafternoon which could, once again, result in some some convective \ntype snow showers. Snow, rain, graupel, and even some thunder \ncannot be ruled out Friday afternoon for the western half of the \nforecast area. \n\nThis weekend temperatures will have no problem rebounding back\nabove average. Behind the high amplitude trough pushing through\nthe CONUS a ridge will build into the intermountain west. This\nwill bring temps back to average Saturday before eclipsing that \nSunday. Actually, each day through early to mid next week wil see\nhigher temperatures than the day previous. Highs in the low 80s F\nare becoming increasingly likely for locations near and just east\nof Billings Monday and Tuesday. \n\nLate next week there is a good signal that another impactful \nsystem could move through our area. Specific details remain \nuncertain, however, there is good consensus that something will be\nimpacting western North America. Some models have the upper level\nlow getting completely cutoff and meandering off of the Pacific \nCoast before it eventually gets pushed eastwards; which would \ndelay any impacts here. Other solutions show a progressive \npattern continuing with this feature manifesting as a longwave\ntrough. More details will come into view as we get closer to the \npotential event. WMR",
